# AMBE-3000F worked example packets, transcribed from the manufacturer.
#
# Source: AMBE-3000F Vocoder Chip Users Manual, version 3.7, October 2016,
# section 6.11, Tables 111 to 114, printed pages 84 to 87.
#
# One record per line: a name, then the packet as hex. Comments start with '#'.
#
# These are the manufacturer's own bytes and they are the definition of the
# framing. Every one of the four computes exactly under the rule in section
# 6.5.2 -- the length counts the field bytes and the parity bytes and excludes
# the four header bytes -- which is why they are here rather than a paraphrase
# of the rule.
#
# **Two of the four disagree with the prose printed beneath them, and the
# tables are right.** Under Table 111 the text says the length is 0x0144, 324
# bytes of fields and 328 in total; the table says 0x0143, and 0x40 plus
# 0x00 0xA0 plus 320 is 323. Under Table 114 the text says 0x0010 and 16
# bytes; the table says 0x000F, and the fields add to 15. Both are off by one
# in the same direction, and the two remaining examples -- Table 112 at 0x0149
# and Table 113 at 0x000C -- compute with no discrepancy at all. So the
# arithmetic is not in doubt; the prose is.
#
# The speech data here is the 160-sample ramp from 0 to 159 that the manual's
# own text describes. The printed hex in Table 111 carries a stray digit
# around sample 0x15, so the printed data cannot be used to recover a byte
# count -- another reason the length field is the authority.

speech-example-1 

speech-example-2 

channel-example-1 61000C01015000112233445566778899

channel-example-2 61000F014001380011223344556603A1020000
